  "contracts": {
    "lem-centered-independent-weighted-sum-variance": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-independent-random-elements",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ be random elements on a\ncommon probability space. For each $i$, write\n\n$$\\sigma(X_i):=\\{X_i^{-1}(B):B\\in\\Sigma_i\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal F.$$\n\nThe family $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ is **independent** when the sigma-algebras\n$(\\sigma(X_i))_{i\\in I}$ are independent in the sense of\nIndependent sigma-algebras and independent events.\n\nWhen $\\Sigma_i$ is a Borel sigma-algebra, this agrees with the notation\n$\\sigma(X_i)$ from The sigma-algebra generated by a function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-independent-random-elements-have-product-joint-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, and let\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ for $i<n$ be independent\nrandom elements. Define\n\n$$X=(X_0,\\dots,X_{n-1}):\\Omega\\to\\prod_{i<n}S_i.$$\n\nThen $X$ is a random element of\n$\\left(\\prod_{i<n}S_i,\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\Sigma_i\\right)$, and its law is the\nfinite product of the marginal laws:\n\n$$\\mathbb P_X=\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathbb P_{X_i}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-measurable-functions-preserve-independence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be an independent family of random elements\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$. For each $i$, let\n$g_i:(S_i,\\Sigma_i)\\to(T_i,\\mathcal T_i)$ be measurable. Then the family\n$(g_i\\circ X_i)_{i\\in I}$ is independent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-factorization-of-expectations-for-independent-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $X_0,\\dots,X_{n-1}$ be independent real random variables on a\ncommon probability space, and let $g_i:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be Borel\nmeasurable for each $i<n$.\n\n1. If every $g_i$ is nonnegative, then\n   $$\\mathbb E\\left[\\prod_{i<n}g_i(X_i)\\right]=\\prod_{i<n}\\mathbb E[g_i(X_i)]$$\n   in $[0,+\\infty]$. In this finite product of expectations, a zero factor\n   makes the product zero even when another factor is $+\\infty$; otherwise\n   multiplication has its usual extended nonnegative meaning.\n2. If every $g_i(X_i)$ is integrable, then $\\prod_{i<n}g_i(X_i)$ is integrable\n   and the same factorization holds in $\\mathbb R$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-arithmetic-and-lattice-operations-preserve-measurability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A)$ be a measurable space and let\n$f,g:X\\to\\overline{\\mathbb R}$ be measurable for the Borel sigma-algebra on\nthe extended real line. Products of functions use\n$\\odot_X$ from A total pointwise product for extended-real-valued functions, and $cf$\nabbreviates $\\underline c\\odot_X f$ for the constant function $\\underline c$.\nThe magnitude function is $|f|=\\max(f,-f)$ as in\nThe positive and negative parts of a function. Then:\n\n1. $cf$ is measurable for every real scalar $c$;\n2. $\\max(f,g)$, $\\min(f,g)$, $|f|$, $f^+$, and $f^-$ are measurable;\n3. if the partial pointwise sum $f+g$ is defined at every point, then it is measurable;\n4. the pointwise product $fg:=f\\odot_X g$ is measurable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-cauchy-schwarz-for-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $X,Y\\in L^2(\\mathbb P)$ are real random variables, then\n$$\\mathbb E[|XY|]\\le\\big(\\mathbb E[X^2]\\big)^{1/2}\\big(\\mathbb E[Y^2]\\big)^{1/2}.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if at least one of $X,Y$ is zero almost surely, or\nthere is a constant $c>0$ with\n$$|X|^2=c\\,|Y|^2\\qquad\\mathbb P\\text{-almost surely}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space.\n\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to[0,+\\infty]$ is measurable, its **expectation** is the\n  extended-valued integral\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ or $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C$ is integrable in the sense\n  of Integrable real and complex functions, and their integrals, its\n  **expectation** is again\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P,$$\n  now a finite real or complex number.\n\nFor an integrable real random variable,\n$$\\mathbb E[X]=\\mathbb E[X^+]-\\mathbb E[X^-],$$\nwhere $X^+$ and $X^-$ are its positive and negative parts.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real random variable on a probability space.\n\n- For $p>0$, the **$p$th absolute moment** of $X$ exists when $|X|^p$ is\n  integrable, and is then\n  $$\\mathbb E[|X|^p].$$\n- If $X$ is integrable, its **mean** is $\\mathbb E[X]$.\n- If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n  $$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are square-integrable real random variables on the same\nprobability space, their **covariance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1.1",
          "claim": "Each $W_i$ and $X_i$ is integrable by [F6] applied with the constant random variable $1$. Independence of the two random elements gives independence of $W_i$ and $X_i$ by [F1], so [F4] yields $\\mathbb E[W_iX_i]=\\mathbb E[W_i]\\mathbb E[X_i]=0$. Linearity [F9] over the finite index set gives $\\mathbb E[S]=0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "F9"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1.2",
          "claim": "Fix $i\\ne j$. The coordinate-product maps $(w,x)\\mapsto w_iw_j$ and $(w,x)\\mapsto x_ix_j$ are measurable by [F5]. Since the joint law of $(W,X)$ is the product law by [F2], these maps, one applied to each independent factor, give independent random variables $A=W_iW_j$ and $B=X_iX_j$ by [F3]. Both are integrable by [F6]. Also $\\mathbb E[A]=\\mathbb E[W_i]\\mathbb E[W_j]=0$ by [F4] and mutual independence of the weights. Hence [F4] gives $\\mathbb E[W_iW_jX_iX_j]=\\mathbb E[AB]=0$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1.3",
          "claim": "For each $i$, $W_i^2$ and $X_i^2$ are independent nonnegative random variables by [F2] and [F3]. Applying the nonnegative case of [F4] gives $\\mathbb E[W_i^2X_i^2]=\\mathbb E[W_i^2]\\mathbb E[X_i^2]=\\sigma_i^2\\mathbb E[X_i^2]<\\infty$.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2.1",
          "claim": "Expanding the finite square $S^2$ gives diagonal terms from step 1.3 and cross terms from step 1.2, so $S^2$ is integrable. Linearity [F9] gives $\\mathbb E[S^2]=\\sum_i\\sigma_i^2\\mathbb E[X_i^2]$. By step 1.1, $\\mathbb E[S]=0$; [F7] and [F8] therefore identify this second moment with $\\operatorname{Var}(S)$ and prove the claim.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
