    "lem-symmetric-rectifier-moments": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-change-of-variables-for-expectation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S,\\Sigma)$ be a random element, let\n$\\mathbb P_X$ be its law, and let $g:(S,\\Sigma)\\to\\mathbb R$ or\n$g:(S,\\Sigma)\\to\\mathbb C$ be measurable.\n\n1. If $g\\ge0$, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[g(X)]=\\int_S g\\,d\\mathbb P_X.$$\n2. If $g(X)$ is integrable, then $g$ is integrable with respect to\n   $\\mathbb P_X$ and the same formula holds:\n   $$\\mathbb E[g(X)]=\\int_S g\\,d\\mathbb P_X.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space.\n\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to[0,+\\infty]$ is measurable, its **expectation** is the\n  extended-valued integral\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ or $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C$ is integrable in the sense\n  of Integrable real and complex functions, and their integrals, its\n  **expectation** is again\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P,$$\n  now a finite real or complex number.\n\nFor an integrable real random variable,\n$$\\mathbb E[X]=\\mathbb E[X^+]-\\mathbb E[X^-],$$\nwhere $X^+$ and $X^-$ are its positive and negative parts.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real random variable on a probability space.\n\n- For $p>0$, the **$p$th absolute moment** of $X$ exists when $|X|^p$ is\n  integrable, and is then\n  $$\\mathbb E[|X|^p].$$\n- If $X$ is integrable, its **mean** is $\\mathbb E[X]$.\n- If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n  $$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are square-integrable real random variables on the same\nprobability space, their **covariance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-cauchy-schwarz-for-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $X,Y\\in L^2(\\mathbb P)$ are real random variables, then\n$$\\mathbb E[|XY|]\\le\\big(\\mathbb E[X^2]\\big)^{1/2}\\big(\\mathbb E[Y^2]\\big)^{1/2}.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if at least one of $X,Y$ is zero almost surely, or\nthere is a constant $c>0$ with\n$$|X|^2=c\\,|Y|^2\\qquad\\mathbb P\\text{-almost surely}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1.1",
          "claim": "Symmetry gives $\\mathbb P(Z>0)=\\mathbb P(Z<0)$, and these probabilities sum to $1-p_0$, so each is $(1-p_0)/2$. The gate takes the values $1,a,c$ on the positive, negative, and zero events, respectively, so its squared expectation is $((1+a^2)/2)(1-p_0)+c^2p_0$. For the remaining moment claims assume $\\mathbb E[Z^2]<\\infty$. Applying [F1] and [F2] to $t\\mapsto t^2\\mathbf 1_{t>0}$ and $t\\mapsto t^2\\mathbf 1_{t<0}$ shows that the positive-side and negative-side second moments are equal. Their sum is $\\mathbb E[Z^2]$, since the contribution at zero is zero, so each is $\\frac12\\mathbb E[Z^2]$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1.2",
          "claim": "By [F1], symmetry gives $\\mathbb E[Z]=\\mathbb E[-Z]$, while scalar linearity [F5] gives $\\mathbb E[-Z]=-\\mathbb E[Z]$, hence $\\mathbb E[Z]=0$. The expectation $\\mathbb E[|Z|]$ is finite by [F4]. The positive and negative parts of $Z$ have equal expectations by symmetry and sum to $|Z|$, so linearity [F5] gives each expectation as $\\frac12\\mathbb E[|Z|]$. Therefore $\\mathbb E[\\rho(Z)]=\\frac12\\mathbb E[|Z|]$ and $\\mathbb E[\\phi_a(Z)]=\\mathbb E[Z\\mathbf 1_{Z>0}]+a\\mathbb E[Z\\mathbf 1_{Z<0}]=\\frac{1-a}{2}\\mathbb E[|Z|]$, using [F5] for the sum and scalar factor.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2.1",
          "claim": "The identities $\\rho(Z)^2=Z^2\\mathbf 1_{Z>0}$ and $\\phi_a(Z)^2=Z^2\\mathbf 1_{Z>0}+a^2Z^2\\mathbf 1_{Z<0}$ now give the two asserted second moments from step 1.1.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3.1",
          "claim": "Expanding the variance definition [F3] using expectation linearity [F5] gives $\\operatorname{Var}(\\rho(Z))=\\mathbb E[\\rho(Z)^2]-(\\mathbb E[\\rho(Z)])^2$. Substitution of steps 2.1 and 1.2 proves the stated centered-variance identity and completes all formulas.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
