    "thm-relu-backward-variance-recurrence": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-feedforward-neural-network",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix integers $d,r\\ge1$. A finite feedforward computation graph from $\\mathbb R^d$ to $\\mathbb R^r$ consists of a finite directed graph $(V,E)$ with no directed cycle; pairwise disjoint input, hidden, and output vertex sets $I,H,O$ whose union is $V$; exactly $d$ input vertices and $r$ output vertices; and a specified ordering of $V$ in which the tail of every directed edge precedes its head. The input vertices are labelled bijectively by the coordinates $1,\\ldots,d$, and input vertex $i$ has value $x_i$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Each non-input vertex $v$ has a real bias $b_v$, a real weight $w_{uv}$ for every edge $u\\to v$, and a real activation function $\\rho_v$. In the specified order, its value is\n\n$$z_v=\\rho_v\\!\\left(b_v+\\sum_{u:\\,u\\to v}w_{uv}z_u\\right).$$\n\nThe network output is the vector of values at $O$. A layered multilayer perceptron is the specialization with layers $V_0,\\ldots,V_L$, where $V_0=I$, $V_L=O$, and every edge goes from $V_{\\ell-1}$ to $V_\\ell$ for some $\\ell$; the hidden vertices use a shared activation and the output vertices use the identity activation. A fully connected layered perceptron includes every edge between consecutive layers. The fixed ordering makes the recursive evaluation unambiguous and is part of the graph data.\n\nThe diagram shows the edge direction and evaluation convention for a fully connected scalar-output perceptron with one hidden layer. Each circle is one scalar-valued node; the hidden nodes apply $\\sigma$ and the output node is linear. The diagram illustrates the definition and is not used in any proof.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, node distance=1.5cm]\n  \\node[draw,circle] (x1) {$x_1$};\n  \\node[draw,circle,below of=x1] (x2) {$x_2$};\n  \\node[draw,circle,right of=x1,yshift=-0.35cm] (h1) {$\\sigma$};\n  \\node[draw,circle,below of=h1] (h2) {$\\sigma$};\n  \\node[draw,circle,right of=h1,yshift=-0.35cm] (y) {$y$};\n  \\draw[->] (x1) -- (h1);\n  \\draw[->] (x1) -- (h2);\n  \\draw[->] (x2) -- (h1);\n  \\draw[->] (x2) -- (h2);\n  \\draw[->] (h1) -- (y);\n  \\draw[->] (h2) -- (y);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-symmetric-rectifier-moments",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Z$ be a real random variable whose law is symmetric about zero, meaning\nthat $Z$ and $-Z$ have the same distribution.\nWrite $p_0=\\mathbb P(Z=0)$. Define the ReLU function $\\rho(t)=\\max(t,0)$ and,\nfor $a\\in\\mathbb R$, define $\\phi_a(t)=t$ for $t>0$ and $\\phi_a(t)=at$ for\n$t\\le0$. Then $\\mathbb P(Z>0)=\\mathbb P(Z<0)=(1-p_0)/2$. If\n$\\mathbb E[Z^2]<\\infty$, the following ReLU moment identities hold:\n$$\\mathbb E[\\rho(Z)]=\\frac12\\mathbb E[|Z|],\\qquad \\mathbb E[\\rho(Z)^2]=\\frac12\\mathbb E[Z^2].$$\nThe corresponding PReLU identities are\n$$\\mathbb E[\\phi_a(Z)]=\\frac{1-a}{2}\\mathbb E[|Z|],\\qquad \\mathbb E[\\phi_a(Z)^2]=\\frac{1+a^2}{2}\\mathbb E[Z^2].$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(\\rho(Z))=\\frac12\\mathbb E[Z^2]-\\frac14\\bigl(\\mathbb E[|Z|]\\bigr)^2.$$\nFor any specified value $c\\in\\mathbb R$ at the kink, put\n$\\eta_{a,c}(t)=\\mathbf 1_{t>0}+a\\mathbf 1_{t<0}+c\\mathbf 1_{t=0}$.\nThen, without a moment hypothesis on $Z$,\n$$\\mathbb E[\\eta_{a,c}(Z)^2]=\\frac{1+a^2}{2}(1-p_0)+c^2p_0.$$\nThe zero-at-kink ReLU backpropagation gate is $\\eta_{0,0}$, with second\nmoment $(1-p_0)/2$. It agrees almost surely with the ordinary ReLU derivative\nif $p_0=0$. A specified value at zero is a backpropagation convention, and is\nnot an ordinary derivative there unless $a=1$ and $c=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-centered-independent-weighted-sum-variance",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$. Let $W_1,\\ldots,W_n$ be mutually independent real random\nvariables with finite second moments, means $\\mathbb E[W_i]=0$, and variances\n$\\sigma_i^2$. Let $X=(X_1,\\ldots,X_n)$ be a real random vector whose\ncoordinates have finite second moments, and suppose the random elements $W$\nand $X$ are independent. The coordinates of $X$ need not be mutually\nindependent. For $S=\\sum_{i=1}^n W_iX_i$, the variable $S$ is square-integrable,\n$\\mathbb E[S]=0$, and\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(S)=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\sigma_i^2\\mathbb E[X_i^2].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-factorization-of-expectations-for-independent-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $X_0,\\dots,X_{n-1}$ be independent real random variables on a\ncommon probability space, and let $g_i:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be Borel\nmeasurable for each $i<n$.\n\n1. If every $g_i$ is nonnegative, then\n   $$\\mathbb E\\left[\\prod_{i<n}g_i(X_i)\\right]=\\prod_{i<n}\\mathbb E[g_i(X_i)]$$\n   in $[0,+\\infty]$. In this finite product of expectations, a zero factor\n   makes the product zero even when another factor is $+\\infty$; otherwise\n   multiplication has its usual extended nonnegative meaning.\n2. If every $g_i(X_i)$ is integrable, then $\\prod_{i<n}g_i(X_i)$ is integrable\n   and the same factorization holds in $\\mathbb R$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real random variable on a probability space.\n\n- For $p>0$, the **$p$th absolute moment** of $X$ exists when $|X|^p$ is\n  integrable, and is then\n  $$\\mathbb E[|X|^p].$$\n- If $X$ is integrable, its **mean** is $\\mathbb E[X]$.\n- If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n  $$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are square-integrable real random variables on the same\nprobability space, their **covariance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "cor-cauchy-schwarz-for-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $X,Y\\in L^2(\\mathbb P)$ are real random variables, then\n$$\\mathbb E[|XY|]\\le\\big(\\mathbb E[X^2]\\big)^{1/2}\\big(\\mathbb E[Y^2]\\big)^{1/2}.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if at least one of $X,Y$ is zero almost surely, or\nthere is a constant $c>0$ with\n$$|X|^2=c\\,|Y|^2\\qquad\\mathbb P\\text{-almost surely}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-independent-random-elements-have-product-joint-law",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, and let\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ for $i<n$ be independent\nrandom elements. Define\n\n$$X=(X_0,\\dots,X_{n-1}):\\Omega\\to\\prod_{i<n}S_i.$$\n\nThen $X$ is a random element of\n$\\left(\\prod_{i<n}S_i,\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\Sigma_i\\right)$, and its law is the\nfinite product of the marginal laws:\n\n$$\\mathbb P_X=\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathbb P_{X_i}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1.1",
          "claim": "At a realization with nonzero preactivations, the derivative of ReLU is the diagonal matrix with entries $\\gamma_{\\ell,j}$, and the affine derivative is $W_\\ell$. The chain rule [F2] therefore gives the two backward identities in the Statement. The specified convention defines the same identities at zeros without using [F2] there.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1.2",
          "claim": "For fixed $i$, $g_{\\ell-1,i}=\\sum_jT_{ji}$. A finite sum of square-integrable variables is square-integrable: $(\\sum_jT_{ji})^2\\le d_\\ell\\sum_jT_{ji}^2$. By [F7] each pairwise product is integrable. Expanding the mean and centered square, then applying [F6] and expectation linearity [F9], gives exactly the asserted mean and covariance sums, without factoring any dependent variables.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1.3",
          "claim": "Under the conditional assumptions, [F3] and gate independence give $\\mathbb E[h_{\\ell,j}^2]=\\mathbb E[\\gamma_{\\ell,j}]\\mathbb E[g_{\\ell,j}^2]=(1-p_\\ell)G_\\ell/2$ by [F5]. For fixed $i$, the column of weights is centered, independent, and independent of $h_\\ell$. Applying [F4] yields mean zero and variance $\\sigma_\\ell^2\\sum_j\\mathbb E[h_{\\ell,j}^2]=((1-p_\\ell)/2)d_\\ell\\sigma_\\ell^2G_\\ell$. This also equals the upstream second moment, proving the one-step formula.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2.1",
          "claim": "Repeat step 1.3 for the finitely many layers $L,L-1,\\ldots,r$. All generated upstream coordinates are centered and have the common second moment in step 1.3, so substitution gives the stated product, including a possibly uncentered terminal signal. Setting $p_\\ell=0$ gives the original half-factor formulas and setting $\\sigma_\\ell^2=2/d_\\ell$ makes those factors one.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3.1",
          "claim": "To prove the feasibility obstruction, let $q=\\mathbb P(W_{\\ell,11}<0)$. Symmetry and positive variance imply $q>0$: if $q=0$, symmetry gives $\\mathbb P(W_{\\ell,11}>0)=0$, forcing variance zero. By [F8], the event $E$ that all $d_\\ell d_{\\ell-1}$ matrix entries are strictly negative has probability $q^{d_\\ell d_{\\ell-1}}>0$. Since $X_{\\ell-1}$ is coordinatewise nonnegative, all coordinates of $Z_\\ell$ are nonpositive on $E$, so $\\gamma_\\ell=0$ and $h_\\ell=0$ there, including when the input vector is zero. Independence of $W_\\ell$ and $h_\\ell$ now gives $0=\\mathbb P(E\\cap\\{h_\\ell\\ne0\\})=\\mathbb P(E)\\mathbb P(h_\\ell\\ne0)$, hence $h_\\ell=0$ almost surely. If $p_\\ell<1$, step 1.3's gate calculation gives $0=\\mathbb E[h_{\\ell,j}^2]=(1-p_\\ell)G_\\ell/2$, hence $G_\\ell=0$. This proves the obstruction without declaring the valid conditional identities false.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F8",
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
