    "ex-zero-mean-or-bias-breaks-relu-half-moment": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-symmetric-rectifier-moments",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Z$ be a real random variable whose law is symmetric about zero, meaning\nthat $Z$ and $-Z$ have the same distribution.\nWrite $p_0=\\mathbb P(Z=0)$. Define the ReLU function $\\rho(t)=\\max(t,0)$ and,\nfor $a\\in\\mathbb R$, define $\\phi_a(t)=t$ for $t>0$ and $\\phi_a(t)=at$ for\n$t\\le0$. Then $\\mathbb P(Z>0)=\\mathbb P(Z<0)=(1-p_0)/2$. If\n$\\mathbb E[Z^2]<\\infty$, the following ReLU moment identities hold:\n$$\\mathbb E[\\rho(Z)]=\\frac12\\mathbb E[|Z|],\\qquad \\mathbb E[\\rho(Z)^2]=\\frac12\\mathbb E[Z^2].$$\nThe corresponding PReLU identities are\n$$\\mathbb E[\\phi_a(Z)]=\\frac{1-a}{2}\\mathbb E[|Z|],\\qquad \\mathbb E[\\phi_a(Z)^2]=\\frac{1+a^2}{2}\\mathbb E[Z^2].$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(\\rho(Z))=\\frac12\\mathbb E[Z^2]-\\frac14\\bigl(\\mathbb E[|Z|]\\bigr)^2.$$\nFor any specified value $c\\in\\mathbb R$ at the kink, put\n$\\eta_{a,c}(t)=\\mathbf 1_{t>0}+a\\mathbf 1_{t<0}+c\\mathbf 1_{t=0}$.\nThen, without a moment hypothesis on $Z$,\n$$\\mathbb E[\\eta_{a,c}(Z)^2]=\\frac{1+a^2}{2}(1-p_0)+c^2p_0.$$\nThe zero-at-kink ReLU backpropagation gate is $\\eta_{0,0}$, with second\nmoment $(1-p_0)/2$. It agrees almost surely with the ordinary ReLU derivative\nif $p_0=0$. A specified value at zero is a backpropagation convention, and is\nnot an ordinary derivative there unless $a=1$ and $c=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real random variable on a probability space.\n\n- For $p>0$, the **$p$th absolute moment** of $X$ exists when $|X|^p$ is\n  integrable, and is then\n  $$\\mathbb E[|X|^p].$$\n- If $X$ is integrable, its **mean** is $\\mathbb E[X]$.\n- If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n  $$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are square-integrable real random variables on the same\nprobability space, their **covariance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space.\n\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to[0,+\\infty]$ is measurable, its **expectation** is the\n  extended-valued integral\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\n- If $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ or $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C$ is integrable in the sense\n  of Integrable real and complex functions, and their integrals, its\n  **expectation** is again\n  $$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\int_\\Omega X\\,d\\mathbb P,$$\n  now a finite real or complex number.\n\nFor an integrable real random variable,\n$$\\mathbb E[X]=\\mathbb E[X^+]-\\mathbb E[X^-],$$\nwhere $X^+$ and $X^-$ are its positive and negative parts.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1.1",
          "claim": "In the first case, $\\mathbb E[W]=(-1)(2/3)+2(1/3)=0$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(Z)=\\operatorname{Var}(W)=\\mathbb E[W^2]=2$. Since $\\operatorname{ReLU}(Z)$ is $2$ with probability $1/3$ and zero otherwise, $\\mathbb E[\\operatorname{ReLU}(Z)^2]=4/3$, whereas $\\frac12\\operatorname{Var}(Z)=1$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1.2",
          "claim": "The first preactivation has $\\mathbb P(Z>0)=1/3$ and $\\mathbb P(Z<0)=2/3$, so its law is not symmetric despite its zero mean and absence of an atom at zero. The symmetric-preactivation hypothesis of [F1] fails; zero mean alone cannot replace it.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1.3",
          "claim": "In the second case, conditioning on either value of $W$ shows that $WX$ is uniform on $[-2,2]$. Thus $Z=WX+1$ is uniform on $[-1,3]$, with mean $1$ and variance $(3-(-1))^2/12=4/3$; it has no atom at zero.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2.1",
          "claim": "The density of $Z$ is $1/4$ on $[-1,3]$, so $\\mathbb E[\\operatorname{ReLU}(Z)^2]=\\frac14\\int_0^3 z^2\\,dz=9/4$, while $\\frac12\\operatorname{Var}(Z)=2/3$. The nonzero bias has shifted the symmetric preactivation and invalidated the half-moment identity.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
