    "ex-finite-width-iid-relu-stack-has-zero-atom": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-iid-relu-layer-forces-zero-atom-next",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$ and let $Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n$ be independent and identically\ndistributed real random variables whose law is symmetric about zero and has no\natom at zero. Put $X_i=\\operatorname{ReLU}(Z_i)$. For any real random\nvariables $W_1,\\ldots,W_n$ on the same probability space, define the next\nzero-bias preactivation $T=\\sum_{i=1}^n W_iX_i$. Then\n$$\\mathbb P(T=0)\\ge2^{-n}>0.$$\nIn particular, this next preactivation cannot be atomless at zero, regardless\nof the distribution or dependence of the weights. The same conclusion holds\nunder the weaker assumption that the $X_i$ are mutually independent and each\n$Z_i$ is symmetric with no atom at zero; independence of the $Z_i$ is not\nrequired in that formulation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-relu-backward-variance-recurrence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Consider a finite rectified stack in a fully connected layered perceptron,\nwith widths $d_0,\\ldots,d_L$, zero biases,\n$Z_\\ell=W_\\ell X_{\\ell-1}$, and $X_\\ell=\\rho(Z_\\ell)$,\nwhere $\\rho(t)=\\max(t,0)$. For an objective $J$ differentiable as a function\nof the last activation, set $g_L=\\nabla_{X_L}J(X_L)$ and define backward\nsignals by the zero-at-kink convention\n$\\gamma_{\\ell,j}=\\mathbf 1_{Z_{\\ell,j}>0}$,\n$h_\\ell=\\gamma_\\ell\\odot g_\\ell$, and\n$g_{\\ell-1}=W_\\ell^{\\mathsf T}h_\\ell$.\nAt realizations where all relevant preactivations are nonzero, these are\nordinary chain-rule gradients. At zeros they are specified backpropagation\nsignals; no ordinary derivative of ReLU at zero is asserted.\n\nWithout any independence assumptions, fix a layer $\\ell$ and suppose every\n$T_{ji}=W_{\\ell,ji}\\gamma_{\\ell,j}g_{\\ell,j}$ is square-integrable. Then\n$$\\mathbb E[g_{\\ell-1,i}]=\\sum_j\\mathbb E[T_{ji}],\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(g_{\\ell-1,i})=\\sum_j\\operatorname{Var}(T_{ji})+2\\sum_{j<k}\\operatorname{Cov}(T_{ji},T_{ki}).$$\nThis is an exact atom-aware identity for the realized network signals. For any\nspecified bounded measurable random gate vector $q_\\ell$ in place of\n$\\gamma_\\ell$, define $T_{ji}=W_{\\ell,ji}q_{\\ell,j}g_{\\ell,j}$. If every\nredefined $T_{ji}$ is square-integrable, the same mean and covariance formulas\nhold.\n\nFor the conditional recurrence, additionally assume at the layers in question\nthat the entries of $W_\\ell$ are independent with a common symmetric centered\nlaw of finite variance $\\sigma_\\ell^2$, that $Z_{\\ell,j}$ is symmetric with\ncommon zero probability $p_\\ell$, and that the coordinates of $g_\\ell$ have\ncommon finite second moment $G_\\ell$. Assume the gate vector $\\gamma_\\ell$\nis independent of $g_\\ell$ and $W_\\ell$ is independent of $h_\\ell$.\nWrite the fan-out as $\\hat n_\\ell=d_\\ell$. Then\n$$\\mathbb E[g_{\\ell-1,i}]=0,\\qquad G_{\\ell-1}=\\operatorname{Var}(g_{\\ell-1,i})=\\frac{1-p_\\ell}{2}\\hat n_\\ell\\sigma_\\ell^2G_\\ell.$$\nIf these hypotheses hold for every $r\\le\\ell\\le L$, then\n$$G_{r-1}=G_L\\prod_{\\ell=r}^L\\frac{1-p_\\ell}{2}\\hat n_\\ell\\sigma_\\ell^2.$$\nIn particular, the source's atomless conditional case $p_\\ell=0$ gives\n$\\operatorname{Var}(g_{\\ell-1,i})=\\frac12\\hat n_\\ell\\sigma_\\ell^2\\mathbb E[g_{\\ell,1}^2]$\nand the corresponding product of half factors. If the downstream coordinate\nis centered, $G_\\ell$ is its variance. The conditional fan-out choice\n$\\sigma_\\ell^2=2/\\hat n_\\ell$ makes each atomless product factor one.\n\nThese conditional identities do not establish that a nonzero actual-network\ngradient satisfies their hypotheses. Indeed, if $\\ell\\ge2$,\n$X_{\\ell-1}$ is a ReLU output and $\\sigma_\\ell^2>0$, the assumption\n$W_\\ell\\perp h_\\ell$ already forces $h_\\ell=0$ almost surely. If also\n$p_\\ell<1$ and gate independence holds, it forces $G_\\ell=0$.\nThus the nonzero atomless backward preservation model with these full\nindependence assumptions is infeasible for such an actual ReLU layer; the\nconditional implication and product remain valid, and the general covariance\nidentity above supplies the unconditional finite-network formula.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1.1",
          "claim": "The example has $n=2$, so [F1] gives $\\mathbb P(T=0)\\ge2^{-2}=1/4$, independently of the values of $W_1,W_2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-1.2",
          "claim": "In the scalar stack, $Z_1$ is $\\pm\\sqrt2$ with equal probabilities. If $W_1<0$ then $X_1=0$ and $Z_2=0$; if $W_1>0$ then $Z_2$ is $2$ or $-2$ with equal probabilities. Thus the law of $Z_2$ gives probabilities $1/4,1/2,1/4$ to $-2,0,2$, respectively. Both preactivations have mean zero and variance $2$, and the second has zero probability $1/2$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2.1",
          "claim": "Therefore the two first-layer preactivations can be atomless while the next preactivation is not atomless. Requiring the no-atom condition at every layer is incompatible with this finite IID ReLU stack.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-3.1",
          "claim": "By [F2], $g_1=\\sqrt2 R$ exactly when both weights are positive, an event of probability $1/4$; it is zero otherwise. Independence and the centered sign $R$ give mean zero and variance $2(1/4)=1/2$. The atomless coefficient would be $(1/2)(1)(2)\\mathbb E[R^2]=1$. Here $h_2=R$ on the same positive-weight event and zero otherwise: $\\mathbb P(h_2\\ne0\\mid W_2<0)=0$, whereas $\\mathbb P(h_2\\ne0)=1/4$, so weight/gated-signal independence fails. The covariance identity and the specified kink convention remain valid; the atomless conditional recurrence is inapplicable.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
